\details{
The observed statistic is the usual \eqn{n R^2} from the auxiliary regression of
the squared residuals on the regressors. The bootstrap reference distribution,
however, must be generated \strong{under the homoscedastic null} --- otherwise it
inherits the heteroscedasticity the test is looking for and the procedure loses
all power. Each bootstrap sample is therefore built from leverage-standardised,
mean-centred residuals \eqn{r_i = e_i/\sqrt{1-h_{ii}}} (which have a common
variance under \eqn{H_0}) resampled i.i.d. and perturbed by a wild multiplier
\eqn{v_i}, so that \eqn{y_i^* = \hat y_i + r^*_i v_i}. The statistic is recomputed
on each refit and the p-value is \eqn{(1 + \#\{T_b^* \ge T\})/(B + 1)}. In the package's validation study of
the multiplier, with \code{B = 999}, the test held its level under \eqn{H_0} with
heavy-tailed \eqn{t_5} errors but not with skewed ones: with centred
exponential errors and 30, 50 and 100 observations it rejected a true null
hypothesis 8.2\%, 7.3\% and 6.1\% of the time at the 5\% level with the
Rademacher multiplier, and 7.8\%, 7.0\% and 5.9\% with the Mammen multiplier. Under the
alternative the observed statistic is extreme relative to the homoscedastic reference, giving
power.

Each replicate is refitted on the design matrix of the fit, with its
offset, whether that was given as an argument or written in the formula.
The formula is not evaluated again, so a transformed regressor such as
\code{log(x)}, or a transformed response, is refitted on the scale of the fit.
}
\section{Weighted fits}{

A fit with weights \eqn{w_i} is ordinary least squares on
\eqn{\sqrt{w_i}\, y_i}{sqrt(w_i) y_i} and \eqn{\sqrt{w_i}\, x_i}{sqrt(w_i) x_i},
whose errors have constant variance under the null hypothesis, and the
bootstrap works in that regression. The statistic is computed from its
residuals, the Pearson residuals \eqn{\sqrt{w_i}\, e_i}{sqrt(w_i) e_i}, with
the auxiliary regressors on their original scale; the residuals that are
resampled are the Pearson residuals divided by
\eqn{\sqrt{1 - h_{ii}}}{sqrt(1 - h_ii)}, with the leverages of that
regression, and centred; and each replicate refits it. With equal weights
the result is that of the unweighted fit.

The weights are held fixed in every replicate. When they were estimated
from the same data, as by \code{\link[=fitWLS]{fitWLS()}}, the replicates leave that estimation
out, and the test inherits the error of the tests that take the weights as
known. In \code{inst/validation/weighted-fits-size.R}, on data whose standard
deviation is \eqn{\exp(0.3 x)}{exp(0.3 x)}, with the variance function
correctly specified, normal errors and \code{B = 499}, its rejection rate at the
5\% level was 0.086 with 150 observations and 0.105 with 600. In the same
design with the true weights it was 0.054 and 0.048, and with the weights
estimated again in each replicate, which this function does not do, 0.060
and 0.051. \code{\link[=performVarianceFormTest]{performVarianceFormTest()}} allows for the estimation.
}

